From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 72/72] local: rpmsg: glink: stop paying 10s per request to a
 remote that is gone

Containment, not a repair: it does not bring the remote back.

After a cross-boot hibernate restore on x1e80100 at EL2 the ADSP glink edge is
permanently stale, and cannot be recovered from Linux -- rebuilding the edge
(rproc detach/attach, or unregister/re-register of the glink subdev alone)
makes the DSP assert in its own glink core and die. So every consumer behind
that edge -- qcom-battmgr, the thermal zone it backs, audio, pmic_glink/UCSI --
issues an intent request, waits the full 10 s in
qcom_glink_request_intent(), fails, and immediately tries again.

The result is not merely missing features; the machine becomes unusable.
Measured on a resumed system: load average 3.38 with no runnable task, and
upowerd, the thermal kworker and systemd-journald all parked in D state, while
"intent request timed out" repeats every 10.24 s forever.

Count consecutive timeouts per edge and, after three (30 seconds without a
single answer), fail further requests immediately instead of waiting. Any
successful request resets the count, and a re-registered edge starts from zero,
so a remote that genuinely comes back is picked up again.

This is deliberately conservative: it only engages once the remote has ignored
three full 10 s windows, which on healthy hardware does not happen.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
 drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 35 insertions(+), 1 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/qcom_glink_native.c
@@ -126,7 +126,29 @@
 	bool sent_read_notify;
 
 	bool abort_tx;
+
+	/*
+	 * Consecutive intent-request timeouts on this edge. See
+	 * qcom_glink_request_intent().
+	 */
+	atomic_t intent_req_timeouts;
 };
+
+/*
+ * Each intent request that goes unanswered costs the caller a full 10 second
+ * wait. After a cross-boot hibernate restore the ADSP edge is permanently
+ * stale -- the remote cannot be recovered from Linux at all -- so every
+ * consumer (battery, thermal, audio, UCSI) blocks for 10 s, fails, and
+ * immediately tries again, leaving processes parked in D state indefinitely:
+ * upowerd, the thermal kworker and journald were all stuck this way, with a
+ * load average above 3 and no runnable task.
+ *
+ * Three consecutive timeouts is 30 seconds without a single answer; treat the
+ * remote as gone and fail subsequent requests immediately. Any successful
+ * request clears the count, and a re-registered edge starts from zero, so a
+ * remote that does come back is picked up again.
+ */
+#define GLINK_INTENT_REQ_MAX_TIMEOUTS	3
 
 enum {
 	GLINK_STATE_CLOSED,
@@ -1451,6 +1473,11 @@
 
 	mutex_lock(&channel->intent_req_lock);
 
+	if (atomic_read(&glink->intent_req_timeouts) >= GLINK_INTENT_REQ_MAX_TIMEOUTS) {
+		ret = -ETIMEDOUT;
+		goto unlock;
+	}
+
 	WRITE_ONCE(channel->intent_req_result, -1);
 	WRITE_ONCE(channel->intent_received, false);
 
@@ -1473,11 +1500,18 @@
 				 glink->abort_tx,
 				 10 * HZ);
 	if (!ret) {
-		dev_err(glink->dev, "intent request timed out\n");
+		if (atomic_inc_return(&glink->intent_req_timeouts) >=
+		    GLINK_INTENT_REQ_MAX_TIMEOUTS)
+			dev_err(glink->dev,
+				"intent request timed out %d times, treating remote as gone\n",
+				GLINK_INTENT_REQ_MAX_TIMEOUTS);
+		else
+			dev_err(glink->dev, "intent request timed out\n");
 		ret = -ETIMEDOUT;
 	} else if (glink->abort_tx) {
 		ret = -ECANCELED;
 	} else {
+		atomic_set(&glink->intent_req_timeouts, 0);
 		ret = READ_ONCE(channel->intent_req_result) ? 0 : -EAGAIN;
 	}
 
